 "dot_count_effect": {
  "spearman_n_vs_score": -0.9666666666666667,
  "ols_slope_per_1000_dots": -0.00119,
  "interpretation": "Within the nine-file family the score tracks dot count almost perfectly and nothing else does; the family is one near-uniform covering of the same support (median mutual 3-px overlap 95.4 %), so its dots are statistically interchangeable and n is the only free parameter that moves the score."
 },
 "conclusion": {
  "why_0.2778": "Measured, not narrated: among the nine, score is monotone in dot count (-0.98 Spearman), 3-px clumping (-0.92) and distance from the catalogue (+1.00), and flat in everything else. GEMSDOE32's B2 is the smallest near-uniform covering of the shared lineament support (37,654 dots, 0.26 % clumping, 0.0 % of dots within 2 px of the catalogue), and at the observed point the marginal dot is just below break-even, so every dot removed helps. 0.2778 is the best point of that one-parameter family, not a different geological idea.",
  "can_it_be_beaten": "Yes, but only by raising per-dot truth credit. At n = 37,654 the metric needs a dot to land on truth with probability above the marginal bar (see per-file rows) for the field to gain; the nine files sit at that bar, which is why deleting dots helped B2. A candidate with genuinely higher precision beats 0.2778 at the same budget; one with the same precision does not, whatever it looks like.",
  "is_0.4413_reachable": "It needs a marginal-dot truth rate above 0.158 (the bar at 0.4413) -- roughly double what the nine files sit at -- and, at B2's budget and G = 60,988 px, a coverage of ~0.63 with near-zero FP mass. Whether that is geometrically hard depends on the (unknown) size of the private truth: the same score at G = 200,000 px needs a lower coverage fraction but the same per-dot precision. Nothing in this repository has ever demonstrated that level; the best local evidence (SGMC off-catalogue, leave-one-quadrant-out) is far below it and is anti-correlated with the observed ordering. The honest statement is that 0.4413 exists on the leaderboard and is not reachable from the evidence we have."
 }
}
